"relationship": "related_topic",
"comparison": "While NVDA focuses on the demand-side risks of product transitions (inventory reduction, delayed purchases), AVGO discloses the corresponding supply-side cost impacts associated with these manufacturing cycles. NVDA notes that 'The increasing frequency and complexity of newly introduced products could result in quality or production issues that could increase inventory provisions... or result in product delays.' AVGO corroborates this by listing 'inventory adjustments including write-downs for inventory obsolescence' as a primary component of its cost of products sold. This highlights a shared exposure where the complexity of managing multiple architectures simultaneously leads to both demand volatility (NVDA) and increased costs/write-downs (AVGO).",
